1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,820
You know, when you step into a movie theater, you find your seat, and as the lights go down, you're instantly participating in this identical reality with everyone else in that room.

2
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:14,740
Right, yeah. You're all literally looking at the exact same physical screen.

3
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:21,320
Exactly, and you're all hearing the exact same audio mix being pumped through the, you know, the surround sound speakers.

4
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:21,740
Right.

5
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:29,640
Like, if there's a sudden explosion in the film, the person in the front row and the person in the back row, they receive fundamentally the same sensory input.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,740
It's an objective, shared experience.

7
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:41,020
But, and here's the hook, you step out of that theater, and you walk onto a live concert stage, like right up there with the musicians, during a massive show.

8
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:45,900
Oh, and that entire concept of a shared, objective reality just shatters completely.

9
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:46,680
Totally shatters.

10
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:54,500
What is happening on that stage is actually this elaborate, beautifully coordinated acoustic illusion.

11
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,980
It is entirely an illusion, and, I mean, what's fascinating here is that the musicians aren't being tricked.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,580
You know, they are actively weaponizing it.

13
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:09,840
The architecture of that illusion is profoundly important, and not just for, like, audio engineers.

14
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:13,360
It's important for anyone trying to build complex systems in the real world.

15
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,440
Well, welcome to the deep dive.

16
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:19,180
We are taking you straight to the core of a massive idea today.

17
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:19,820
You really are.

18
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,200
Our mission is to shortcut the path to being genuinely well-informed.

19
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:29,300
We want to strip away the noise so you can grasp the actual mechanics of how things work.

20
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:34,620
So today we're looking at this brilliant piece of writing from August 2026.

21
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:42,560
It's by the essayist Flyxion, and it's titled Hall of Monitors, Coordination Without Identical Information.

22
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:43,660
Such a great title.

23
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:44,380
It is.

24
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:47,380
And our entire conversation centers on this premise.

25
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:47,780
Yeah.

26
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:49,260
The live rock band.

27
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,360
You, standing in the audience, think you're sharing an acoustic reality with the people on stage.

28
00:01:54,560 --> 00:02:02,720
Right, but what is physically happening up there, the physical reality, is that not a single person on that stage is hearing what you, the audience member, are hearing.

29
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:03,320
No.

30
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:09,220
Furthermore, not a single person on that stage is hearing what the musician standing, like, three feet away from them is hearing.

31
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,040
The singer's reality is entirely divorced from the bassist's reality.

32
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:17,500
The drummer's world is fundamentally different from the guitarist's world.

33
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:26,860
And the goal of our deep dive today is to explore why this stage engineering trick is actually a grand overarching theory.

34
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,020
It's a blueprint, really.

35
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:36,520
Yeah, a blueprint for how human beings, distributed software, social platforms, and highly complex multi-agent systems coordinate.

36
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,960
And how they do it without ever actually sharing identical information.

37
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:40,520
Yes.

38
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:46,180
So setting the baseline thesis here, which Flyxion just deconstructs masterfully.

39
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:46,580
Completely.

40
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:57,420
The thesis going into this is our inherent societal assumption that fairness or coordination or a functional shared truth absolutely requires giving everyone the exact same feed.

41
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:57,660
Right.

42
00:02:57,740 --> 00:03:01,040
We assume we must all perceive the identical reality to act together.

43
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:01,420
Exactly.

44
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:03,500
We think, you know, sameness equals fairness.

45
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:12,240
But this essay proves why that assumption is not just mathematically and physically wrong, but why building systems based on that assumption is actively dangerous.

46
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:13,260
It's wild.

47
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:21,540
So to truly grasp the theory, we need to completely deconstruct the physical reality of the stage that inspired it.

48
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:26,760
Yeah, we have to look at what is actually being piped into the in-ear monitors of these musicians.

49
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:32,000
Because if you've ever watched a live performance and seen a musician, like casually adjust that little earpiece.

50
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,140
They are tweaking a highly curated version of reality.

51
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:35,580
Exactly.

52
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:36,860
Let's start with the drummer.

53
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:43,460
The audience is getting hit with this massive, overwhelming wall of sound.

54
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:44,140
Right?

55
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:48,440
Screaming guitars, layered synths, crashing cymbals, five vocal harmonies.

56
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:49,360
It's huge.

57
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:56,200
But if you were to walk up to the drummer and say, pop one of their earpieces into your own ear, what does that world sound like?

58
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,320
Honestly, it would sound incredibly bizarre.

59
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:00,720
Like almost unrecognizable as the song you're listening to.

60
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:01,020
Really?

61
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:01,540
Yeah.

62
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:12,060
The drummer is hearing mostly a highly compressed, isolated bass guitar line, the rhythmic click of a metronome, and perhaps a tiny thin thread of the lead vocal.

63
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:12,880
Wow.

64
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:19,860
They are explicitly not hearing the wash of the cymbals they're hitting, and they are definitely not hearing the rich distortion of the rhythm guitar.

65
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,140
Yeah, and why is that?

66
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:27,740
Because, I mean, if I'm the drummer and I hear everything, I actually can't do my job.

67
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:28,460
Exactly.

68
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:33,140
If I hear the wash of noise the audience hears, the subtle variations in the rhythm get muddy.

69
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:34,660
I lose the groove.

70
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:35,000
Right.

71
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:36,320
Your brain is just overloaded.

72
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:36,560
Yes.

73
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:47,380
I need the bass player to anchor the temporal reality of the song, and I need just enough of the lead singer's voice to know if we're, like, entering the chorus or the bridge.

74
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:49,280
Everything else is a distraction.

75
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:50,120
Exactly.

76
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:55,080
The drummer's cognitive load is entirely dedicated to keeping time and structure.

77
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:57,900
Now, contrast that with the lead singer.

78
00:04:58,280 --> 00:05:04,140
If you listen to the singer's monitor mix, you're going to hear their own voice pushed aggressively forward.

79
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:04,820
Oh, I bet.

80
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:14,720
It will be overwhelmingly loud and dry, sitting right at the front of their skull, and alongside that, they might have a single keyboard line or an acoustic guitar.

81
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:18,940
Because the singer doesn't need the pounding, low-end frequencies of the bass and kick drum.

82
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:19,460
No.

83
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:24,160
In fact, those frequencies will actually vibrate their vocal cords and throw off their intonation.

84
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:24,380
Right.

85
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:24,960
Yeah.

86
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:27,240
It physically interferes with their instrument.

87
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:29,880
So, they need a pure melodic reference.

88
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:31,280
A pitch center.

89
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:38,000
They need to hear themselves clearly enough to not blow out their vocal cords trying to compete with the room volume.

90
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:38,600
Precisely.

91
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:42,740
And then you get to the most counterintuitive mix of all, which is the guitarist.

92
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:43,260
Oh, my gosh.

93
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:44,800
This detail blew my mind.

94
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:45,180
Right.

95
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:54,120
If you talk to a professional touring guitarist, a lot of the time, they want almost none of their own instrument in their in-ear monitor.

96
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:55,660
Almost none of it.

97
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:57,360
Which sounds insane.

98
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:58,720
You're playing an instrument.

99
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:01,280
Surely, you need to hear exactly what you're playing.

100
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:08,820
It sounds like a mistake until you break down the physical mechanics of playing the electric guitar on a loud stage.

101
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:09,580
Okay, break it down.

102
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:11,720
Well, first, they already know what their hands are doing.

103
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:12,660
They have muscle memory.

104
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:13,060
Sure.

105
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:18,100
But more importantly, they're standing in front of an amplifier pushing actual air, right?

106
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:18,500
Right.

107
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:22,500
So, they can physically feel the resonance of the instrument against their bottom.

108
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:23,540
Oh, like bone conduction.

109
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:23,800
Yeah.

110
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:24,400
Exactly.

111
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:25,800
They have bone conduction.

112
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:31,800
So, what they desperately lack in that loud environment is spatial and temporal awareness.

113
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:32,800
Oh, yeah.

114
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:40,940
They need the hi-hat of the drum kit to know exactly when to land the downbeat, and they need the vocal to know the emotional dynamic of the section.

115
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:42,480
That makes so much sense.

116
00:06:42,540 --> 00:06:48,340
If their monitor was just their own guitar, they would be a soloist completely divorced from the band.

117
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:57,100
So, if you ask a working musician why their mix sounds absolutely nothing like the ambient room, their answer isn't that they are confused.

118
00:06:57,300 --> 00:06:57,900
No, never.

119
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:00,820
The answer is that the discrepancy is by design.

120
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:08,720
That highly filtered, bizarre-sounding mix is an engineered reality constructed specifically to allow them to execute their role.

121
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:14,940
And what's fascinating here is that Flyxion makes this massive leap from audio engineering to systems architecture.

122
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:15,260
Yeah.

123
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:16,260
This is where it gets big.

124
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:20,060
This stage setup isn't just a quirky byproduct of loud music.

125
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:27,920
It is a working, highly successful example of an architecture that most systems claiming to coordinate people totally fail to build.

126
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:43,280
Whether we're talking about algorithmic news feeds, enterprise software used by multinational corporations, or distributed AI networks, they either fail to grasp this architecture or they actively avoid building it.

127
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:44,480
Okay, let's unpack this.

128
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:48,660
I have to challenge you on the idea that tech platforms actively avoid this.

129
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:49,040
Oh.

130
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:54,160
Because on the surface, this sounds exactly like what Silicon Valley does every single day.

131
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:55,860
They personalize everything.

132
00:07:55,980 --> 00:07:56,440
They do.

133
00:07:56,680 --> 00:08:00,760
My social media feed looks absolutely nothing like your social media feed.

134
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,720
My search results are different from yours.

135
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:07,520
Isn't that the exact same thing as the drummer getting a different mix than the singer?

136
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:09,240
It's a customized feed.

137
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:10,700
It is customized, yes.

138
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:14,500
But the mechanics underneath that customization are entirely different.

139
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:15,000
How's that?

140
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:19,820
What tech platforms are currently doing is like a dark mirror of what the band is doing.

141
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:20,580
Dark mirror.

142
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:20,820
Yeah.

143
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:29,460
The band's setup, no matter how disparate the individual mixes get, strictly maintains a structural, verifiable connection to a single shared reality.

144
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:31,060
The actual song being played.

145
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:33,140
Exactly, the actual song.

146
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:41,320
But the tech platform's setup, as Flyxion argues, frequently severs that connection completely, usually in the name of engagement.

147
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:41,860
Wow.

148
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:42,220
Okay.

149
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:46,420
We are going to dissect the mathematical difference between those two approaches shortly.

150
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:56,840
But that distinction between a healthy, coordinated projection and an isolated, severed reality, that is the beating heart of this text.

151
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:57,400
I see.

152
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:04,740
Well, before we can diagnose exactly where these massive digital systems go wrong, we have to look at the terminology we're using.

153
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:05,200
Definitely.

154
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:10,200
Because to understand what a monitor should be doing, we have to look at the word itself.

155
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:16,340
Flyxion points out a linguistic accident surrounding the word monitor that is actually incredibly revealing.

156
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,540
It tells us so much about human psychology and system design.

157
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:19,780
Yeah.

158
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:28,100
There are four distinct senses of the word monitor in English, and we use them all the time without realizing they describe very different relationships to reality.

159
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:32,680
We use them interchangeably, but structurally, they dictate entirely different power dynamics.

160
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:34,080
Let's walk through the first three.

161
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:46,960
Sense number one, a monitor is an interface or a physical device that a person listens or watches through, like, I'm listening through my stage monitors, or I'm buying a new 4K monitor for my computer.

162
00:09:47,180 --> 00:09:48,560
It is the physical window.

163
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:49,000
Right.

164
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:52,660
Sense number two, a monitor is a display of a state.

165
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:56,760
It's a readout representing a system's condition at a given moment in time.

166
00:09:56,820 --> 00:10:00,260
Like a heart rate monitor in a surgical theater displaying the beats per minute.

167
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:01,240
Perfect example.

168
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,440
And then sense number three is a bit more computational.

169
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:03,920
Right.

170
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:08,280
Sense three refers to a process that watches another process.

171
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:08,540
Okay.

172
00:10:09,040 --> 00:10:12,260
It is an agent checking a system against an expectation.

173
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:21,860
So in computer science, you have monitor programs or demons that run in the background supervising other software to ensure it doesn't leak memory or crash.

174
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:28,640
So you have the physical interface, the representation of the state, and the active process checking for errors or deviations.

175
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:31,400
Now, look back at the musician on the concert stage.

176
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:37,400
Their in-ear monitor is performing all three of those functions simultaneously in perfect harmony.

177
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:38,120
Oh, I see it.

178
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:40,620
It is the physical device they listen through.

179
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:45,360
It is displaying a highly curated version of the performance's current state.

180
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:50,920
And crucially, it allows the musician to perform that third function.

181
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:51,980
Which is?

182
00:10:52,080 --> 00:10:55,020
They are checking their own playing against the rest of the band.

183
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:57,540
They are the process checking the process.

184
00:10:57,760 --> 00:10:58,860
The tool empowers them.

185
00:10:58,960 --> 00:10:59,300
Exactly.

186
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:02,080
They are not merely being watched by the system.

187
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:05,880
They are actively wielding it to participate in a shared objective.

188
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:09,000
But then there is the dark fourth sense of the word.

189
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:13,020
And honestly, this is the one most of us learn first as kids.

190
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:14,960
The institutional use of the word.

191
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:15,600
Exactly.

192
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:22,680
A monitor as a person posted to oversee a room, the middle school hall monitor, or an exam monitor.

193
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:23,020
Right.

194
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:25,020
Think about what that role actually is.

195
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:27,300
They aren't there to help you learn the curriculum.

196
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:30,260
They aren't facilitating your participation in the school's mission.

197
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:32,940
No, their sole function is surveillance and compliance.

198
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:33,420
Yes.

199
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:38,700
They are watching to ensure you don't run, don't cheat, don't deviate from the rules.

200
00:11:39,060 --> 00:11:42,140
It is a purely supervisory, asymmetrical relationship.

201
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:45,220
And Flixine's insight here is foundational.

202
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:48,300
When a system separates those first three functions,

203
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:57,180
when a tech platform or a corporate structure treats what a user sees as completely unrelated to what the system's actual state is,

204
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:01,960
and separates that again from how the system checks for failure coordination degrades.

205
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:03,140
It breaks down.

206
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:03,720
Yes.

207
00:12:03,980 --> 00:12:09,660
When those functions are decoupled, the system devolves into that purely supervisory fourth sense.

208
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,560
The platform is just watching the user.

209
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:12,640
Like it tracking clicks.

210
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:13,520
Exactly.

211
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:18,260
It's tracking your clicks, monitoring your engagement, checking you for gross policy violations,

212
00:12:18,660 --> 00:12:22,460
but it is not empowering you to understand the shared state of the network.

213
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:23,620
It's just a hall monitor.

214
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:24,160
Yes.

215
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:28,880
Uniting those first three functions in a single tool is not a UX convenience.

216
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:36,420
It is an absolute necessity for any system that wants healthy, autonomous coordination among its participants.

217
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:37,540
I love that framing.

218
00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:42,160
A healthy monitor expands an agent's capacity to perceive and act,

219
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:46,700
while a degraded monitor just watches the agent to enforce compliance.

220
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:47,440
All right.

221
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:50,760
So we know what a healthy monitor should be doing conceptually.

222
00:12:51,180 --> 00:12:54,540
Let's look at the actual physics and mathematics of what it transmits to the user.

223
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:56,220
How does it actually work?

224
00:12:56,220 --> 00:13:01,320
This brings us to the core concept of the essay, the shared state.

225
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:04,180
Flyxion uses a very specific notation for this, right?

226
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:04,400
Yeah.

227
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:09,340
The shared state is represented mathematically as a continuing event evolving in time.

228
00:13:09,540 --> 00:13:09,860
Yes.

229
00:13:09,980 --> 00:13:14,260
The essay uses the variable S moving through time T, so S of T.

230
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:20,280
Now, we're not going to read out algebraic formulas on an audio show because that is a total nightmare to listen to.

231
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:20,740
No.

232
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:23,720
But let's translate the math into spatial reality.

233
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:26,640
Imagine S is the entirety of the room.

234
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:35,740
On the concert stage, this is the pure, unmixed, raw sound of every single instrument, every voice, the crowd noise, the reflections off the back wall.

235
00:13:35,900 --> 00:13:41,360
The literal, objective, acoustic truth of the physical space in that exact millisecond.

236
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:44,120
But here is the massive twist that Flyxion introduces.

237
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:46,960
Nobody in the band experiences the room.

238
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:47,880
Not a single person.

239
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:49,220
It's crazy to think about.

240
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:55,420
The pure, unadulterated reality of the room is structurally inaccessible to the people actually creating it.

241
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:57,020
Instead, they receive a projection.

242
00:13:57,020 --> 00:14:07,960
The system takes the totality of the room, applies a strict mathematical filter to it, and projects a highly specific slice of that reality directly into the ear of person A.

243
00:14:08,060 --> 00:14:08,340
Yes.

244
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:12,980
And it throws away 90% of the room and only delivers the 10% that person A needs.

245
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:15,960
And this is where we have to aggressively dismantle the sameness myth, right?

246
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:17,980
Flyxion is so adamant about this.

247
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:34,040
In social design, in political theory, in software architecture, we have this deep-seated, almost invisible assumption that for coordination to be fair or successful, participant A's reality must be perfectly identical to participant B's reality.

248
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:36,100
We assume the projections must match.

249
00:14:36,340 --> 00:14:38,660
Like, M sub I has to equal M sub J.

250
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:39,020
Yes.

251
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:42,740
We treat identical information as the prerequisite for shared truth.

252
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:49,920
Flyxion argues that treating identical projection as the goal of coordination technology is the fundamental failure of modern systems.

253
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:50,940
It was never the goal.

254
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:51,260
No.

255
00:14:51,700 --> 00:15:01,940
The rock band is the ultimate proof that you can have flawlessly coordinated, highly complex, synchronous behavior between agents whose projections of reality share almost nothing in common.

256
00:15:02,300 --> 00:15:03,980
Let's ground this outside of music.

257
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:09,440
Because I was trying to think of an analogy for how this works in, like, daily human interaction.

258
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:10,740
Oh, I'd love to hear it.

259
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:12,320
Think about a group of friends.

260
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:16,520
Who take a massive two-week cross-country road trip together.

261
00:15:16,980 --> 00:15:18,860
They are all in the same car.

262
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:23,560
They are sharing the exact same physical state moving through time.

263
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:24,380
Okay.

264
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:25,080
S of T.

265
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:26,460
The car on the road.

266
00:15:26,700 --> 00:15:27,180
Exactly.

267
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:27,700
Yeah.

268
00:15:27,700 --> 00:15:32,700
Now, imagine a month later, they are at a dinner party telling a story about that trip.

269
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:40,960
If all four of them sat down and repeated the exact same sentences, word for word, describing the exact same details in the exact same order.

270
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:42,260
That would be terrifying.

271
00:15:42,260 --> 00:15:42,780
It would.

272
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:44,840
It wouldn't feel like a shared human memory.

273
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:47,100
It would feel incredibly, deeply unsettling.

274
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:49,160
They would sound like programmed robots.

275
00:15:49,380 --> 00:15:49,660
Right.

276
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:56,440
The richness and the truth of the shared experience actually comes from the fact that their memories, their projections are totally different.

277
00:15:56,580 --> 00:15:57,380
Oh, I see.

278
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:02,080
The person who drove remembers the physical stress of the mountain passes and the sound of the engine.

279
00:16:02,220 --> 00:16:07,660
The passenger remembers the intricate details of the playlists they were curating and the weird billboards.

280
00:16:08,300 --> 00:16:12,660
And the person in the back seat just remembers the angle of the sun and sleeping through Nevada.

281
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:13,100
Yes.

282
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:22,500
Their individual projections are wildly distinct, but they all effortlessly map back to the exact same vehicle moving down the exact same highway.

283
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:24,920
That is a phenomenal analogy.

284
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:30,640
Because what makes that storytelling at the dinner party work isn't that their mental contents are identical.

285
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:33,420
It's that their mental contents are compatible.

286
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:34,420
Compatible.

287
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:34,920
Yes.

288
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:39,920
This is Flyxion's true requirement for coordination, the compatibility condition.

289
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:41,820
So it's not about sameness.

290
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:44,000
It is exclusively about compatibility.

291
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:44,800
Precisely.

292
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:55,820
The whole set of projections, everyone's individual memory of the road trip or every musician's individual monitor mix, must function as a working set of views onto the same underlying event.

293
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:56,100
Right.

294
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:04,140
The drummer's mix and the singer's mix are radically different, but they are both faithful, usable guides back to the architecture of the song.

295
00:17:04,140 --> 00:17:08,980
And, you know, when we were prepping for this, I noticed you got completely sidetracked by the footnotes in this section.

296
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:10,300
Oh my gosh, I fully admit it.

297
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:15,260
You were practically vibrating with excitement over the psychological theory backing this up.

298
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:20,500
I really went down a massive rabbit hole here because Flyxion isn't just making this up.

299
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,940
Footnote 1 connects this compatibility condition directly to foundational cognitive psychology.

300
00:17:26,120 --> 00:17:26,820
Who are the researchers?

301
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:32,640
It references Herbert Clark and Susan Brennan's 1991 conversational grounding theory.

302
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:34,280
Okay, what does that say?

303
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:43,660
Well, Clark and Brennan established that successful human communication does not mean two people have identical mental contents.

304
00:17:43,940 --> 00:17:48,040
It doesn't mean I copy a file from my brain and paste it into yours.

305
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:48,840
Thank goodness.

306
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:49,240
Right.

307
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:54,180
Communication rests on a mutually maintained common ground that gets updated turn by turn.

308
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:55,380
I say a sentence.

309
00:17:55,900 --> 00:17:59,720
You process it through your unique context, adjust your internal model, and reply.

310
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:06,260
I hear your reply, realize you maybe slightly misunderstood my tone, and I adjust my next sentence to correct it.

311
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,520
It's a continuous dynamic coupling.

312
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:12,780
It is a shared activity of navigation, not a static copying of data.

313
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:14,500
Which maps perfectly back onto the stage.

314
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:18,100
Like, the drummer plays a slightly aggressive fill heading into the chorus.

315
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:18,460
Yes.

316
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:24,040
The guitarist hears that dynamic shift in their monitor and instinctively digs into their strings a bit harder.

317
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:29,220
The singer feels the energy rise behind them and pushes the vocal higher.

318
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:35,040
They are updating their shared reality turn by turn based on compatible but distinct inputs.

319
00:18:35,840 --> 00:18:37,080
And there was another footnote, right?

320
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:38,420
About the math.

321
00:18:38,540 --> 00:18:38,820
Yes.

322
00:18:39,060 --> 00:18:42,320
Footnote 2 gets into the mathematical nuance of how we measure this.

323
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:48,380
Fushin notes that this compatibility condition isn't just a simple threshold number you can measure on a spreadsheet.

324
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:49,340
What is it then?

325
00:18:49,340 --> 00:18:51,460
It's a topological condition.

326
00:18:52,060 --> 00:18:57,140
Think of it as phase locking, or path consistency across overlapping local views.

327
00:18:57,220 --> 00:18:58,400
Okay, I have to stop you there.

328
00:18:59,140 --> 00:19:04,400
Topological phase locking is a terrifying phrase for anyone who hasn't taken advanced physics.

329
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:05,540
Huh. Fair enough.

330
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:09,480
Let's break down the mechanism of what that actually means in the physical world.

331
00:19:09,640 --> 00:19:09,860
Oh.

332
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:15,020
Phase locking just means that two independent oscillating systems align their rhythms, right?

333
00:19:15,020 --> 00:19:15,760
Yes, exactly.

334
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:20,940
Like two pendulum clocks on the same wall eventually swinging in unison because of the micro vibrations in the wood.

335
00:19:21,020 --> 00:19:21,880
Perfect example.

336
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:28,740
So on stage, the drummer has an internal metronome, and the bassist has an internal metronome.

337
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,240
They're independent systems.

338
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:36,000
But the sound waves of the bass hit the drummer's ear, and the sound waves of the kick drum hit the bassist's ear.

339
00:19:36,120 --> 00:19:36,500
Right.

340
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:42,840
Those distinct views force their internal metronomes to align to lock into the same phase in time.

341
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:45,200
They don't need to share the same melodic information.

342
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:49,560
They just need their temporal realities to lock together so the song doesn't collapse.

343
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:50,900
That is exactly right.

344
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:58,520
They are tethered to the same underlying event through their overlapping edges, even if the center of their interfaces are totally distinct.

345
00:19:58,940 --> 00:19:59,240
Wow.

346
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:01,820
Okay, so this brings us to the terrifying part of the essay.

347
00:20:02,020 --> 00:20:02,860
Ah, yes.

348
00:20:03,140 --> 00:20:04,020
The failure state.

349
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:04,400
Right.

350
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:10,880
If compatibility is the goal, what happens when a system loses its grip on that underlying shared state?

351
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:12,300
What happens when the tether snaps?

352
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:13,120
Gets bad.

353
00:20:13,280 --> 00:20:15,300
This is the core thesis of the entire piece.

354
00:20:16,020 --> 00:20:19,280
The structural difference between a hall of monitors and a hall of mirrors.

355
00:20:19,460 --> 00:20:21,680
This is the most crucial distinction Flyxion makes.

356
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:25,640
In a true hall of monitors, you have a property called recoverability.

357
00:20:25,640 --> 00:20:34,820
Meaning that a participant, armed only with their weird, highly personalized, filtered mix, can still find their way back to the truth of the room.

358
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:35,340
Yes.

359
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:40,960
They can take their in-ear monitor out, listen to the ambient noise of the stage, point to the sound engineer and say,

360
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:43,940
Hey, the bass is rushing the tempo, give me more snare drum.

361
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:44,500
Right.

362
00:20:44,600 --> 00:20:52,340
They fundamentally know that they are experiencing a partial view of reality, and they have a mechanism to verify it against the whole.

363
00:20:52,500 --> 00:20:54,760
The path back to the shared state is recoverable.

364
00:20:54,760 --> 00:20:55,320
Exactly.

365
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:59,700
And there's a beautiful paradox embedded in this recoverability.

366
00:21:00,840 --> 00:21:02,560
Discarding information is not a flaw.

367
00:21:02,820 --> 00:21:03,020
Really?

368
00:21:03,300 --> 00:21:06,180
Discarding information is the entire point of the system.

369
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:10,420
A good monitor is what mathematicians call a lossy projection.

370
00:21:11,040 --> 00:21:15,720
It deliberately collapses a relevant distinction so the participant isn't overwhelmed.

371
00:21:16,040 --> 00:21:17,500
Oh, like equivalence relations.

372
00:21:17,780 --> 00:21:18,060
Yes.

373
00:21:18,420 --> 00:21:21,200
Flyxion uses the concept of equivalence relations here.

374
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:30,700
For the drummer, the exact tone of the rhythm guitar, whether the guitarist is using a fuzz pedal or a clean tone, does not matter for the purpose of keeping time.

375
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:31,800
It's irrelevant data.

376
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:34,120
So the monitor throws that data away.

377
00:21:34,400 --> 00:21:37,740
It treats a fuzzy guitar and a clean guitar as equivalent states.

378
00:21:38,320 --> 00:21:38,940
This is good.

379
00:21:39,260 --> 00:21:41,880
It reduces the drummer's cognitive load.

380
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:47,540
But wait, if throwing away data is good, how does this beautiful functional system fail?

381
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:49,700
Here's where it gets really interesting.

382
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:55,240
It fails through a concept borrowed directly from signal processing and digital imaging called aliasing.

383
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:55,920
Aliasing?

384
00:21:55,920 --> 00:21:56,260
Yeah.

385
00:21:56,860 --> 00:22:05,180
A functional monitor discards irrelevant information, but it fails structurally when it discards crucial distinctions, creating a false equivalence.

386
00:22:05,720 --> 00:22:07,160
What do you mean by false equivalence?

387
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:16,520
It's when two underlying states in the real world that actually require totally different responses from the user are flattened by the system and made to look identical.

388
00:22:16,800 --> 00:22:19,000
I need an example of that because that sounds a bit abstract.

389
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:22,600
Walk me through how aliasing actually tricks a user.

390
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:26,500
Okay, let's use digital photography first, as it's the most common example of aliasing.

391
00:22:27,180 --> 00:22:38,020
Have you ever taken a picture of someone wearing a finely striped shirt, and in the digital photo, the shirt suddenly has these weird, swirling, wavy patterns all over it that aren't there in real life?

392
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:39,920
Oh yeah, the waré effect.

393
00:22:40,120 --> 00:22:41,500
It completely ruins the photo.

394
00:22:41,600 --> 00:22:42,880
Exactly, the waré effect.

395
00:22:43,540 --> 00:22:46,760
The digital camera sensor has a specific grid of pixels.

396
00:22:47,340 --> 00:22:49,920
The physical shirt has a very tight grid of stripes.

397
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:50,420
Right.

398
00:22:50,420 --> 00:22:57,980
When the frequency of the stripes exceeds the resolution of the camera sensor, the camera can't accurately record the data, so it basically guesses.

399
00:22:58,440 --> 00:22:58,720
Ah.

400
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:03,820
It aliases the high-frequency stripes down into a low-frequency, wavy pattern.

401
00:23:04,100 --> 00:23:05,720
It creates a false reality.

402
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:07,680
Wow, okay, now apply that to the band.

403
00:23:08,180 --> 00:23:13,740
Imagine the band suddenly speeds up the tempo by 10 beats per minute because the crowd is going wild.

404
00:23:14,260 --> 00:23:16,920
This change requires the drummer to play faster.

405
00:23:17,140 --> 00:23:17,620
Obviously.

406
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:25,600
But imagine the drummer's monitor mix is being run through a faulty algorithm that heavily compresses and time-stretches the audio to keep everything sounding uniform.

407
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:26,300
Oh no.

408
00:23:26,960 --> 00:23:28,500
The underlying state changed.

409
00:23:28,920 --> 00:23:33,000
It demands a physical response, but the projection collapsed that distinction.

410
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:37,620
The fast tempo and the slow tempo were aliased to sound identical.

411
00:23:37,620 --> 00:23:39,080
So the drummer doesn't speed up.

412
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:39,420
Right.

413
00:23:39,660 --> 00:23:45,160
The drummer has no way of knowing their reality was flattened, so they keep playing slow, and the song train racks.

414
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:46,480
That is wild.

415
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:49,160
The system didn't just hide information.

416
00:23:49,660 --> 00:23:51,900
It actively hallucinated a false stability.

417
00:23:52,140 --> 00:23:52,400
Yes.

418
00:23:52,660 --> 00:23:58,420
And that mechanism aliasing leads directly to what Flyxion calls the descent into the hall of mirrors.

419
00:23:58,480 --> 00:23:58,620
Right.

420
00:23:58,620 --> 00:23:59,620
This is where it gets dark.

421
00:23:59,780 --> 00:24:05,540
Flyxion decals this architectural sequence where a healthy system mutates into a toxic one.

422
00:24:06,480 --> 00:24:10,920
In a healthy system, the shared state, the room, generates a mix for the user.

423
00:24:11,340 --> 00:24:12,540
S goes to M sub J.

424
00:24:12,540 --> 00:24:21,460
But in a failing system, instead of constantly referring back to the room, the system starts building the next projection off the previous projection.

425
00:24:21,980 --> 00:24:24,220
It feeds its own output back into its input.

426
00:24:24,460 --> 00:24:25,960
It's the snake eating its own tail.

427
00:24:26,440 --> 00:24:27,180
Why does this happen?

428
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:34,320
It happens because locally, step by step, it feels to the system designers like a highly efficient optimization.

429
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:34,980
Awesome.

430
00:24:35,360 --> 00:24:38,700
Think about a recommendation algorithm on a social video platform.

431
00:24:38,700 --> 00:24:43,140
On day one, it watches what you click on, and it builds tomorrow's feed based on those clicks.

432
00:24:43,220 --> 00:24:43,580
Okay, fine.

433
00:24:43,700 --> 00:24:43,800
Sure.

434
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:49,580
But then it builds the next day's feed based on how you interacted with the feed it explicitly designed for you.

435
00:24:49,660 --> 00:24:50,360
Oh, I see it.

436
00:24:50,540 --> 00:24:53,520
It is no longer projecting the shared state of the world.

437
00:24:54,020 --> 00:24:57,460
It is projecting your reaction to its previous projection.

438
00:24:57,680 --> 00:24:59,020
It's losing the tethered S.

439
00:24:59,200 --> 00:24:59,640
Exactly.

440
00:24:59,640 --> 00:25:09,220
It is refining itself based entirely on your past behavior within the closed loop, not on the objective state of external reality.

441
00:25:09,700 --> 00:25:16,840
So locally, to a product manager looking at a chart, it feels like the system is getting more tailored, more useful, more engaging.

442
00:25:17,140 --> 00:25:20,940
But globally, the shared reality vanishes completely.

443
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:24,920
This raises an important question, though, because this is the invisible failure.

444
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:27,340
A hall of monitors has windows.

445
00:25:27,820 --> 00:25:29,520
You can look through your mix and see the room.

446
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:33,200
A hall of mirrors only has surfaces reflecting surfaces.

447
00:25:33,720 --> 00:25:37,920
Your feed reflects your past choices, which reflects your past choices infinitely.

448
00:25:38,740 --> 00:25:42,580
And the most terrifying part of this architecture, you cannot tell you were inside it.

449
00:25:42,600 --> 00:25:43,360
No, you can't.

450
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:46,740
There is no alarm bell that rings when a monitor degrades into a mirror.

451
00:25:47,080 --> 00:25:49,380
You don't feel a sudden sense of isolation.

452
00:25:49,900 --> 00:25:54,700
In fact, it feels incredibly comfortable because the environment is perfectly molded to your prejudices.

453
00:25:54,700 --> 00:25:55,640
It's frictionless.

454
00:25:55,980 --> 00:26:04,400
Yeah, you just quietly lose the capacity to ask what the actual room sounded like because the concept of the room no longer exists in your interface.

455
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:05,320
It just disappears.

456
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:13,760
That sends a chill down my spine because you know exactly how a tech executive from a major social platform would respond to this criticism.

457
00:26:13,840 --> 00:26:14,400
Oh, absolutely.

458
00:26:14,760 --> 00:26:16,400
They would immediately jump on the defensive.

459
00:26:16,580 --> 00:26:17,280
They'd say, wait a minute.

460
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:18,820
We are not a hall of mirrors.

461
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:21,540
We keep all the original data on our servers.

462
00:26:21,540 --> 00:26:27,800
We have flawless logs of every single post, every article, every video ever uploaded to our network.

463
00:26:28,100 --> 00:26:29,560
We haven't lost the shared state.

464
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:31,580
It is sitting in a data center in Oregon.

465
00:26:31,860 --> 00:26:32,280
Exactly.

466
00:26:32,680 --> 00:26:38,780
And what I love about this essay is that Flyxion anticipates this exact defense and dismantles it entirely.

467
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:40,840
It is a profound dismantling.

468
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:45,780
Flyxion introduces a crucial distinction here, referencing a companion essay they wrote.

469
00:26:46,180 --> 00:26:49,840
Retained by the system is not equal to recoverable by the participant.

470
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:53,860
Let me push back on you here just to play out the tech executive's argument.

471
00:26:54,060 --> 00:26:54,760
Sure, go for it.

472
00:26:54,940 --> 00:27:01,020
Because logically, if a platform has a perfect, incorruptible internal log of the shared state,

473
00:27:01,140 --> 00:27:07,100
why does it matter if the end user can't easily see the raw chronological firehose of data?

474
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:07,460
Right.

475
00:27:07,820 --> 00:27:10,320
The objective truth still exists on the server.

476
00:27:10,760 --> 00:27:13,000
The algorithm is just organizing it to be helpful.

477
00:27:13,480 --> 00:27:14,360
It hasn't been deleted.

478
00:27:14,740 --> 00:27:17,660
It matters because of a concept called epistemic fragmentation.

479
00:27:17,660 --> 00:27:23,520
A system can be a perfect archive and simultaneously a terrible haul of coordination.

480
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:24,780
How does that work?

481
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:30,880
Well, if the platform has superb internal recoverability, meaning their engineers can run a SQL query

482
00:27:30,880 --> 00:27:35,580
and see the raw data, but zero external recoverability for the user,

483
00:27:35,980 --> 00:27:38,720
you leave the participant utterly trapped in their projection.

484
00:27:38,840 --> 00:27:40,360
Because they can't access it themselves.

485
00:27:40,620 --> 00:27:45,800
The user has no independent structural position from which to measure their own drift from reality.

486
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:51,360
If I cannot practically access the raw feed to see how my personalized feed differs from it,

487
00:27:51,480 --> 00:27:54,380
then my personalized feed is my entire functional world.

488
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:55,460
That makes total sense.

489
00:27:55,860 --> 00:28:01,980
The fact that the truth exists on a server in Oregon is entirely mathematically useless

490
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:05,400
to my ability to coordinate my actions with my fellow citizens.

491
00:28:06,140 --> 00:28:09,120
The system hoards the truth for its own oversight,

492
00:28:09,600 --> 00:28:12,880
but denies the user the navigational tools to find it.

493
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:18,460
The analogy that came to mind here is the difference between a public library and a prison cell with a book slot.

494
00:28:19,060 --> 00:28:20,180
Oh, that's dark, but yeah.

495
00:28:20,280 --> 00:28:23,500
In a public library, I might spend all my time in the science fiction section.

496
00:28:23,640 --> 00:28:25,120
That's my preference, my projection.

497
00:28:25,600 --> 00:28:32,620
But I can physically stand up, look across the room, and see the biography section, the history section, the reference desk.

498
00:28:32,820 --> 00:28:35,200
You have a map of the total shared state.

499
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:41,340
Exactly. The modern tech platform is like being locked in a room where a librarian slides books under the door

500
00:28:41,340 --> 00:28:43,540
based purely on what you read yesterday.

501
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:44,940
That is precisely the difference.

502
00:28:45,060 --> 00:28:47,680
The books exist. The library building exists.

503
00:28:48,260 --> 00:28:49,700
But you have no map of it.

504
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:52,640
You have no way to verify if history books even exist anymore.

505
00:28:52,840 --> 00:28:53,180
Yes.

506
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:57,240
Flyxion contrasts the systemic failure with the companion essay.

507
00:28:57,700 --> 00:29:03,240
That earlier essay was about how a single individual might lose their way in a mediated reality.

508
00:29:03,240 --> 00:29:05,360
But this essay is about architecture.

509
00:29:05,840 --> 00:29:08,620
It's about the literal floor plan of the system itself.

510
00:29:09,420 --> 00:29:09,740
The blueprint.

511
00:29:09,960 --> 00:29:13,920
The question is, did the architects ever build way home in the first place?

512
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:18,280
If the route back to the shared state isn't practically available to the participant,

513
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:22,680
if the UI hides it, if the algorithm actively suppresses it,

514
00:29:22,860 --> 00:29:25,480
then the architecture is fundamentally a hall of mirrors,

515
00:29:25,840 --> 00:29:29,020
regardless of how many petabytes of data they retain on their back end.

516
00:29:29,020 --> 00:29:35,980
Okay, so if we accept that giving everyone access to a recoverable reality is the goal of a good system,

517
00:29:36,260 --> 00:29:38,760
we run into a massive logistical problem.

518
00:29:39,080 --> 00:29:39,500
What's that?

519
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:43,400
How does a system decide who gets what specific mix to begin with?

520
00:29:43,680 --> 00:29:45,520
How do you construct the initial projection?

521
00:29:46,740 --> 00:29:52,260
According to Flyxion, system designers are completely conflating two entirely different computational problems.

522
00:29:52,260 --> 00:29:58,080
This is perhaps the most practical, actionable insight in the entire piece for anyone building software.

523
00:29:58,620 --> 00:30:02,860
There are two distinct scheduling problems in any collaborative environment,

524
00:30:03,220 --> 00:30:05,080
and naive systems smash them together.

525
00:30:05,360 --> 00:30:06,480
Production and perception.

526
00:30:06,700 --> 00:30:07,960
Let's break those down deeply.

527
00:30:08,760 --> 00:30:13,500
First, production, which the essay denotes as the graph of production.

528
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:18,320
This is simply about who realizes which component of the shared state.

529
00:30:18,320 --> 00:30:18,720
Right.

530
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:20,200
Who plays the guitar?

531
00:30:20,780 --> 00:30:21,900
Who sings the lead vocal?

532
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:24,280
Who keeps the time on the drum kit?

533
00:30:24,540 --> 00:30:28,960
It's an assignment problem based on affinity, skill, or resource allocation.

534
00:30:29,460 --> 00:30:35,160
You assign the drum part to the person who has the physical stamina and rhythmic skill to play the drums.

535
00:30:35,280 --> 00:30:35,500
Right.

536
00:30:35,620 --> 00:30:36,780
It's the division of labor.

537
00:30:36,960 --> 00:30:39,640
It answers the question, who is generating the signal?

538
00:30:39,800 --> 00:30:40,600
And the other graph.

539
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:43,280
But then you have the completely separate graph of perception.

540
00:30:43,280 --> 00:30:48,420
This answers the question, who needs to perceive which component in order to do their job well?

541
00:30:48,740 --> 00:30:57,240
And the fatal conflation is that most naive personalization software, especially early machine learning models, collapses these two graphs into one.

542
00:30:57,380 --> 00:30:57,780
Exactly.

543
00:30:58,200 --> 00:31:03,020
The algorithm assumes that whoever is producing a signal must be the one who most needs to perceive it.

544
00:31:03,200 --> 00:31:08,500
Which, if we go back to our master metaphor of the stage, is demonstrably hilariously false.

545
00:31:08,660 --> 00:31:09,000
It is.

546
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:11,260
The drummer does not sing the lead vocal line.

547
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:13,560
The drummer is not producing that signal.

548
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:22,040
But the drummer absolutely desperately needs to hear the vocal line in their monitor to understand the macro structure of the song.

549
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:22,540
Yes.

550
00:31:22,940 --> 00:31:29,500
If you collapse production and perception, the algorithm would say, well, the singer is producing vocals, so only feed vocals to the singer.

551
00:31:29,720 --> 00:31:33,640
The drummer is producing percussion, so only feed percussion to the drummer.

552
00:31:33,920 --> 00:31:39,100
The band would collapse into total rhythmic chaos in less than 10 seconds.

553
00:31:39,200 --> 00:31:39,720
Instantly.

554
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:40,100
Okay.

555
00:31:40,260 --> 00:31:40,860
I love this.

556
00:31:40,860 --> 00:31:53,180
And I want to dive into the exact mechanics of how you solve this perception problem, because you pull some fascinating context from the footnotes regarding how this actually works physically in the ear.

557
00:31:53,360 --> 00:31:54,220
Yeah, I did.

558
00:31:54,360 --> 00:31:56,820
You introduced this concept of the noise floor.

559
00:31:57,040 --> 00:31:57,340
Yes.

560
00:31:57,740 --> 00:32:02,860
This is where Flyxion gets incredibly granular, but it's essential for understanding why algorithms fail.

561
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:07,840
Every participant in a system has a local detection threshold known as a noise floor.

562
00:32:08,040 --> 00:32:08,760
A noise floor.

563
00:32:08,760 --> 00:32:11,280
Below that floor, a signal might be physically real.

564
00:32:11,400 --> 00:32:15,920
It might be transmitted perfectly by the system, but it doesn't exist cognitively to that person.

565
00:32:16,140 --> 00:32:25,660
So if the vocal line is playing in the drummer's monitor, but it's below their noise floor because the bass is so loud, the drummer cannot use it to keep time.

566
00:32:25,860 --> 00:32:27,460
It is functionally non-existent.

567
00:32:27,540 --> 00:32:27,980
Exactly.

568
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:29,840
I want to make sure I understand this mechanically.

569
00:32:30,180 --> 00:32:33,680
Let's say I'm the sound engineer, and the drummer says, I can't hear the singer.

570
00:32:33,680 --> 00:32:38,260
My instinct is just to grab the master volume knob and turn everything up.

571
00:32:38,500 --> 00:32:38,680
Sure.

572
00:32:38,860 --> 00:32:40,840
Make the whole mix uniformly louder.

573
00:32:41,300 --> 00:32:43,560
But you're saying that doesn't fix the problem.

574
00:32:43,780 --> 00:32:47,440
It absolutely does not fix the problem, and in fact, it usually makes it worse.

575
00:32:47,680 --> 00:32:47,940
Why?

576
00:32:47,940 --> 00:32:53,460
Because if you just crank the master volume, you raise the noise floor along with the signal.

577
00:32:53,720 --> 00:32:56,620
You make the bass louder, the click track louder, the vocal louder.

578
00:32:56,920 --> 00:33:00,160
The vocal is still buried underneath the competing frequencies.

579
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:02,580
It's just vibrating the eardrum harder.

580
00:33:02,580 --> 00:33:03,020
Ah.

581
00:33:03,740 --> 00:33:06,700
You just deafen the musician without providing any clarity.

582
00:33:07,380 --> 00:33:09,980
So perception routing isn't about volume.

583
00:33:10,620 --> 00:33:12,920
It's about selective threshold manipulation.

584
00:33:13,740 --> 00:33:14,300
Exactly.

585
00:33:14,620 --> 00:33:20,460
It's about intelligently raising a very specific signal, like the vocal cue, just enough to

586
00:33:20,460 --> 00:33:25,080
cross the drummer's specific threshold of perception at a specific moment in the song

587
00:33:25,080 --> 00:33:29,300
while actively suppressing or letting other competing signals recede.

588
00:33:29,760 --> 00:33:34,420
And again, you pulled some amazing historical context from footnotes four and five about where

589
00:33:34,420 --> 00:33:35,880
this theory actually comes from.

590
00:33:36,520 --> 00:33:40,120
Flyxion is drawing on decades of intense scientific research here.

591
00:33:40,200 --> 00:33:41,900
I really couldn't resist going deep on this.

592
00:33:41,900 --> 00:33:46,780
Footnote 4 traces this back to human automation research from the 1970s and 1990s.

593
00:33:46,800 --> 00:33:47,460
Who were the pioneers?

594
00:33:47,460 --> 00:33:51,280
People like Thomas Sheridan, Bill Verplank, and Christopher Wickens.

595
00:33:51,860 --> 00:33:56,860
They studied how to allocate tasks in high-stress environments between human operators and early

596
00:33:56,860 --> 00:33:57,680
computer systems.

597
00:33:58,500 --> 00:34:00,360
But Flyxion points out their blind spot.

598
00:34:00,920 --> 00:34:06,380
Even they frequently fail to cleanly separate who produces an action from who perceives the

599
00:34:06,380 --> 00:34:07,540
feedback of that action.

600
00:34:07,720 --> 00:34:08,580
They conflated them.

601
00:34:08,580 --> 00:34:11,040
Yes, they treated allocation as a single variable.

602
00:34:11,880 --> 00:34:17,180
Flyxion is arguing that separating those two graphs is the fundamental key to modern coordination.

603
00:34:17,460 --> 00:34:18,840
What about footnote 5?

604
00:34:18,940 --> 00:34:20,580
This was the psychoacoustic stuff, right?

605
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:21,320
Yes.

606
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:29,260
Footnote 5 traces the noise floor concept back to 1966, to David Green and John Swetz's signal

607
00:34:29,260 --> 00:34:35,520
detection theory, and even further back to Harvey Flyxion's groundbreaking 1933 work on critical

608
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:36,000
bands.

609
00:34:36,500 --> 00:34:36,900
1933.

610
00:34:37,220 --> 00:34:38,180
That's almost 100 years ago.

611
00:34:38,220 --> 00:34:38,580
I know.

612
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:43,000
Flyxion was a physicist at Bell Labs, and he proved mathematically that a tone isn't

613
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:44,580
drowned out by all the noise in a room.

614
00:34:44,840 --> 00:34:49,560
It is only drowned out by a very narrow band of noise frequencies immediately surrounding it.

615
00:34:49,560 --> 00:34:53,180
So if you want somebody to hear a 1,000 hertz tone, you don't need a silent room.

616
00:34:53,300 --> 00:34:57,320
Joe, you just need to carve out the noise between 900 and 1,100 hertz.

617
00:34:57,480 --> 00:34:57,620
Wow.

618
00:34:58,160 --> 00:35:04,780
Flyxion is taking this incredibly specific physical acoustic phenomenon and applying it to general

619
00:35:04,780 --> 00:35:06,780
socio-technical system architecture.

620
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:09,240
The algorithm doesn't need to show you everything.

621
00:35:09,460 --> 00:35:13,940
It just needs to carve out the specific cognitive frequencies competing with the signal you need

622
00:35:13,940 --> 00:35:14,480
right now.

623
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:16,700
It is a brilliant extrapolation.

624
00:35:16,700 --> 00:35:21,160
It really is. But translating that to software brings up a massive practical issue.

625
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:25,220
We've established we need to constantly manipulate this noise floor for each person.

626
00:35:25,560 --> 00:35:27,600
But how does a system do that?

627
00:35:27,700 --> 00:35:28,080
It's hard.

628
00:35:28,220 --> 00:35:33,260
Is this just a settings menu? Like, I open my app, check some preference boxes, say show

629
00:35:33,260 --> 00:35:37,820
me more news, show me less sports, and then the system just runs on autopilot forever.

630
00:35:38,240 --> 00:35:42,820
Not at all. And this is the trap that almost every modern consumer platform falls into.

631
00:35:42,940 --> 00:35:45,060
Flyxion calls this the myth of the profile.

632
00:35:45,060 --> 00:35:50,820
Right. Most modern personalization works by building a static historical preference profile.

633
00:35:51,160 --> 00:35:51,420
Yes.

634
00:35:51,740 --> 00:35:56,160
The algorithm looks at what you clicked on yesterday, last week, last month, and builds

635
00:35:56,160 --> 00:36:03,540
a dossier. User 405 likes cat videos, baking tutorials, and political outrage. We will weigh

636
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:08,020
those variables heavily and feed them exactly that cocktail forever.

637
00:36:08,020 --> 00:36:12,860
But a live monitor engineer on a concert stage does not work that way. A professional monitor

638
00:36:12,860 --> 00:36:17,060
console is not a static profile. It is a highly dynamic instrument.

639
00:36:17,200 --> 00:36:18,080
It's constantly changing.

640
00:36:18,200 --> 00:36:23,120
The weights of the mix are changing continuously in real time. When the singer has a really

641
00:36:23,120 --> 00:36:28,080
tough, exposed vocal entrance come up in the bridge of a song, the engineer doesn't just

642
00:36:28,080 --> 00:36:33,160
leave the mix alone. The engineer might push a specific keyboard pitch reference forward

643
00:36:33,160 --> 00:36:37,920
in the singer's ear for exactly three seconds leading up to the entrance. And the millisecond,

644
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:42,880
the singer hits the note securely. The engineer pulls that keyboard back down so it doesn't

645
00:36:42,880 --> 00:36:44,560
clutter the rest of the vocal performance.

646
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:45,060
No.

647
00:36:45,300 --> 00:36:49,900
The monitor responds to the demands of the present moment and the requirements of the immediate

648
00:36:49,900 --> 00:36:54,380
future, not a historical average of what the singer listened to last week.

649
00:36:54,380 --> 00:36:59,300
Let me throw a real provocation at you, though. Because we live in a consumer-driven society.

650
00:36:59,740 --> 00:37:01,880
What if the drummer just wants to hear more vocals?

651
00:37:02,180 --> 00:37:03,900
Oh, this happens all the time.

652
00:37:03,960 --> 00:37:07,520
Not because they need it for a musical cue, but simply because they like the sound of the

653
00:37:07,520 --> 00:37:14,320
singer's voice. Shouldn't a personalized system give the user exactly what they prefer to consume?

654
00:37:14,620 --> 00:37:16,940
Isn't that the definition of a good user experience?

655
00:37:17,100 --> 00:37:22,000
That is the ultimate temptation of the algorithmic age. But Flickshenand lays out what I consider

656
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:24,600
the absolute golden rule of this entire essay.

657
00:37:24,760 --> 00:37:25,460
Okay. What is it?

658
00:37:25,460 --> 00:37:31,980
System designers must ask this specific question. Which projection of the shared state best preserves

659
00:37:31,980 --> 00:37:34,560
this participant's next admissible continuation?

660
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:41,420
The next admissible continuation. That is such a heavy, dense phrase. What does that actually mean

661
00:37:41,420 --> 00:37:43,360
in practice? Unpack that for me.

662
00:37:43,480 --> 00:37:49,380
It means we have to stop optimizing systems for immediate, frictionless preference or enjoyment.

663
00:37:49,580 --> 00:37:49,800
Okay.

664
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:55,160
If you are the monitor engineer and you optimize only for what the drummer likes to hear, you

665
00:37:55,160 --> 00:38:00,360
crank the vocals. But by doing that, you raise the noise floor and drown out the bass line.

666
00:38:00,440 --> 00:38:02,020
And the bass line is what they actually need.

667
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:06,700
The bass line is the specific signal the drummer needs to perceive to keep the rhythm for the

668
00:38:06,700 --> 00:38:13,600
next 16 bars. Optimizing for immediate comfort, eventually, mathematically, optimizes away the

669
00:38:13,600 --> 00:38:17,680
very cues that keep a person capable of continuing their task in the shared world.

670
00:38:17,680 --> 00:38:22,560
That is profound. A healthy monitor is not a historical profile of your past desires.

671
00:38:23,040 --> 00:38:27,760
It is a dynamic control surface that equips you to successfully execute future actions.

672
00:38:27,920 --> 00:38:32,400
Exactly. A static profile traps you in a feedback loop of your past preferences.

673
00:38:32,820 --> 00:38:36,360
A dynamic control surface equips you with the capacity for future action.

674
00:38:36,360 --> 00:38:43,640
And a platform that only feeds you what you like is actively disabling your capacity to navigate the

675
00:38:43,640 --> 00:38:50,880
friction of the shared world. It is slowly blinding you to the signals you need to function as a citizen.

676
00:38:51,120 --> 00:38:51,940
It really is.

677
00:38:52,060 --> 00:38:54,520
Okay. This complex dynamic recovering system,

678
00:38:54,720 --> 00:39:00,960
the true hall of monitors, sounds incredible when it works. It's responsive. It's tailored. It keeps

679
00:39:00,960 --> 00:39:04,560
everyone coordinated without forcing them into identical realities.

680
00:39:04,640 --> 00:39:06,100
When it works, it's beautiful.

681
00:39:06,420 --> 00:39:12,060
But it begs a massive structural question. Who ensures it doesn't break down? Who audits the

682
00:39:12,060 --> 00:39:15,640
algorithm to make sure the monitor hasn't quietly mutated into a mirror?

683
00:39:15,880 --> 00:39:19,240
This is what Flyxion terms the crisis of ambient verification.

684
00:39:19,240 --> 00:39:24,280
The crisis of ambient verification. The essay points out the ultimate cheat code of the

685
00:39:24,280 --> 00:39:27,420
Ross Band metaphor, right? The physical room itself. The air.

686
00:39:27,480 --> 00:39:28,240
The air is free.

687
00:39:28,380 --> 00:39:33,560
The acoustic environment provides free ambient verification. If the monitor mix goes completely

688
00:39:33,560 --> 00:39:38,680
haywire, anyone, the sound tech, the bass player, a fan in the front row, can simply pull their

689
00:39:38,680 --> 00:39:43,160
earpiece out, listen to the open air, and check the shared state against what the musicians are doing.

690
00:39:43,820 --> 00:39:48,820
Exactly. The objective truth is literally vibrating the air molecules in the room. You can't fake it.

691
00:39:49,240 --> 00:39:54,300
But if we connect this to the bigger picture of modern society, this is where the hammer drops.

692
00:39:54,460 --> 00:39:55,200
It drops hard.

693
00:39:55,340 --> 00:40:01,640
Distributed software, personalized social media feeds, massive algorithmic tech platforms.

694
00:40:02,340 --> 00:40:03,840
They have no room air.

695
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:07,940
None. There is no ambient acoustic environment you can casually listen to.

696
00:40:08,160 --> 00:40:12,700
You cannot casually look past your own algorithmic feed to see the true state of the network.

697
00:40:12,820 --> 00:40:15,040
The network only exists through the interface.

698
00:40:15,180 --> 00:40:15,440
Yes.

699
00:40:15,640 --> 00:40:18,740
That is the sharpest, most chilling warning in the text.

700
00:40:19,240 --> 00:40:22,340
A hall of mirrors doesn't trigger a system failure alarm.

701
00:40:22,860 --> 00:40:25,420
The platform doesn't flash red when it traps you.

702
00:40:25,420 --> 00:40:31,280
It just quietly, efficiently keeps amplifying what is already loud and easy to justify the

703
00:40:31,280 --> 00:40:35,720
things you already click on, the opinions you already hold, the outrage you already feel.

704
00:40:35,800 --> 00:40:40,920
Meanwhile, the quiet, crucial, complex signals that you actually need to understand the nuances

705
00:40:40,920 --> 00:40:43,300
of the world never cross your noise floor.

706
00:40:43,300 --> 00:40:43,860
Right.

707
00:40:44,360 --> 00:40:46,720
They aren't explicitly censored by a dictator.

708
00:40:46,900 --> 00:40:50,260
They just never get amplified enough by the algorithm for you to perceive them.

709
00:40:50,520 --> 00:40:52,060
They're drowned out by your own preferences.

710
00:40:52,540 --> 00:40:55,240
And this brings us all the way back to the beginning of our conversation.

711
00:40:55,720 --> 00:40:58,740
The return of the dark fourth sense of the word monitor.

712
00:40:58,960 --> 00:40:59,420
Oh, wow.

713
00:40:59,640 --> 00:40:59,800
Yeah.

714
00:40:59,800 --> 00:41:04,300
Without that ambient verification, without the room air to check reality against the

715
00:41:04,300 --> 00:41:08,820
system, degrades entirely into its purely supervisory function.

